{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":281291,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"cec0dd27-a1d6-4245-aa33-57607a030bdb","total_cost_usd":1.2248824999999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":64431,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"output_tokens":21071,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":64431,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":21071,"cache_read_input_tokens":16967,"cache_creation_input_tokens":64431,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":64431},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":45209,"outputTokens":19,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.045304,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":21071,"cacheReadInputTokens":16967,"cacheCreationInputTokens":64431,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.1795784999999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"playtester\",\n  \"verdict\": \"targets_missed\",\n  \"summary\": \"The base ship never reaches the band — every ascent crashes with an apex of ~5.1 km against a 50,000 m floor — and Armstrong's module cannot be landed by any route at any shield tier (best measured peak 545–567 against a 226 capacity ceiling); both trace to a 105 kg dry mass that leaves the ship drag-limited on the way up and 34× overloaded on the way down.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ascent_crashes_before_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.5\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.5 states the base ship reaches the band floor on a ballistic arc with 29.5% of tank left and a 97-second EVA window; balance_notes claim ~24% margin at the re-authored flight numbers and a launch TWR of 3.3.\",\n      \"measured\": \"All three sample ascents report reached=false, why='crashed on ascent', arc_apex_m 5068 / 5104 / 5116 against a band floor of 50,000 m — a factor of ten short. arc_eva_window_s 0, arc_fuel_margin_pct 0, fuel_remaining_kg 0 at every sample, by both routes. The controlling quantity is the unstaged ballistic coefficient: 175 kg wet over Cd 0.4 x 3.5 m2 = 125 kg/m2 at liftoff, flown into a 1.5 kg/m3 sea-level atmosphere, so drag (0.5 x 1.5 x v^2 x 0.4 x 3.5 = 1.05 v^2) equals the full 5200 N thrust at only 70 m/s and equals thrust-minus-weight at 59 m/s. The vehicle is thrust-limited by air, not by delta-v: its 2,213 m/s ideal budget is spent below 6 km. The exploration grid satisfies bands_reachable in 93.2% of 5,184 configurations, so this is a value that is wrong, not a target that cannot be hit.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"module_cannot_come_home\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.2 and 2.6 make the finale an aerobraking flight: the module peaks at 268.8 on a plunge and 196.5 on one braking pass, against a maxed capacity near 235, so it comes home on one skim. balance_notes claim 344.7 plunge / 216.5 on two skims against a 222/224/226 ladder.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Flown with the commit floor at 8,000 m, the module (3,600 kg) peaks at 603.4 / 726.6 / 909.9 on a plunge from bottom / middle / top, and its best skimmed descent anywhere is 545.3 / 567.0 / 561.0. skim_saves_it is false at every sample and survives is false in all twelve module cells. Against heat_capacity 222 and a top tier of 226, the coolest descent available to the module is 2.41x the best shield in the game. The win condition is not merely hard, it is unreachable. Every module figure in both the GDD and the balance notes is low by 2.2x-3.4x, while the empty ship (158.3 vs 144.7) and full hold (223.6 vs 203.7) land within 10% of the GDD's table — the error is concentrated entirely in the mass ratio, not in the heat model.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mass_rules_mutually_unsatisfiable\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.4\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.4 requires a full hold to roughly double the ship's mass; GDD 2.6 requires the 3,600 kg module to roughly double the ship's mass. game_params sets dry_mass_kg 105 to satisfy the first against a catalog averaging 17.36 kg per slot.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Both cannot hold. The flown catalog gives kg_per_slot 17.36 and full_hold_kg 104.1, so the doubling rule pins dry mass at 105 kg — lighter than the 110 kg suit it carries, with a staged ballistic coefficient of 21.4 kg/m2 against the sweep's own note that below ~50 the atmosphere stops the ship rather than braking it, and against the Researcher's assumed 600 kg staged dry mass. At 105 kg the module is 34x the ship and 18x a full hold, not 2x. The visible costs are measured, not argued: the module's peak heat runs 3.4x the GDD's figure, the parachute ladder has to span 72 to 1,150 m2 (16x) to land both a 209 kg return and a 3,705 kg finale, and full_hold_mass_ratio 1.99 is the only rule the number actually buys. Satisfying both GDD sentences needs a ~1,800 kg ship with a ~300 kg-per-slot hold, or a module of a few hundred kg. No value of dry_mass_kg resolves it; this is a GDD/catalog decision, not a tuning move.\",\n      \"severity\": \"blocking\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"full_hold_forced_to_skim_from_top\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.2 is explicit that an empty ship and a full hold still plunge freely, and warns 'do not try to force the full hold to skim as well.' balance_notes claim a full hold plunges from the top of the band with 62% margin (86.4 against 222).\",\n      \"measured\": \"The full hold's committed plunge from the top of the band peaks at 223.6 against heat_capacity 222 — survives=false, must_skim=true, a margin of minus 0.7%. The balance notes' mass-rescaled figure of 86.4 is low by 2.6x. The half hold clears at 193.8 (13% margin) and the empty ship at 158.3 (29%), so the design's stated intent survives at every load except the one it explicitly named. This is the capacity ladder failing, not the mechanic: at any capacity above ~250 the full hold plunges again.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"unstaged_braking_pass_is_never_a_choice\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.2 presents staged versus unstaged braking as 'a real choice' — 3x heat for the hull in exchange for keeping the engine and the ability to move periapsis between passes.\",\n      \"measured\": \"An unstaged shallow pass peaks at 417 (bottom) and 401 (middle) against a capacity of 222 — survivable=false at both, by 88% and 81%. The choice does not exist in the shipped numbers: unstaged is uniformly fatal, so every descent is a staged descent and the periapsis-steering half of the mechanic is dead. The grid satisfies unstaged_pass_survivable in 22.8% of configurations, so it is reachable — but note that none of the thirteen top-scoring configurations satisfies it, and best_score is 8 of 9 across all 5,184: this target is the one every high scorer trades away. Raising capacity into the module window below would incidentally restore it with ~28% margin.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"one_pass_is_cheapest_everywhere\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"claim\": \"params ablation.optimal_skims sets bottom 0, middle 0, top 1, i.e. one braking pass pays for itself from the top of the band. GDD Revision 3 withdrew the older 'cheapest descent should be 2 passes' goal as unsatisfiable.\",\n      \"measured\": \"The withdrawal is confirmed and the params' top=1 is contradicted. cheapest_pass_count is 1 in all twelve descent cells. At the top of the band a full hold costs 29.2% of plate on one pass against 55.5% on two (1.90x) and 83.3% on three; the empty ship runs 19.1 / 39.5 / 65.8; the module 334.8 / 540.3 / 617.0. Feathering is confirmed unavailable — 25 passes with a full hold from the top costs 12,705,187% of plate, and 11-13 passes cost 5,041-15,407% — so ablation.cycle_toll_growth 1.75 is doing exactly its designed job. Multi-pass is a feasibility rule and never a cost rule; no capacity or ablation keying changes that, and nothing should be tuned to try.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"design\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"skim_benefit_is_band_dependent_and_saturates_at_one\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"claim\": \"params ablation.skim_heat_multiplier is a single array [1, 0.71, 0.63, 0.60]; GDD 2.2 states one skim is worth 0.42-0.53x on the peak with entry depth held fixed, saturating after about two.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Measured at fixed entry depth with the skim altitude scanned, the multiplier is band-dependent and saturates after exactly one skim: bottom [1, 0.716, 0.716, 0.716], middle [1, 0.519, 0.519, 0.519], top [1, 0.382, 0.382, 0.382]. The second and third skims buy literally nothing — identical entry_peak_rate (14.54 / 13.81 / 13.47) and identical peak heat to three figures. The GDD's 0.42-0.53 range is right only at the middle sample; the real spread is 0.38-0.72, and the single tabulated array is wrong at both ends of the band. The mechanic itself is robust: skimming_cools_the_entry holds in 97.2% of the grid and skim_benefit_saturates in 87.3%. Only the number is wrong.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"greed_never_costs_a_landing\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.4\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.4 gives the suit a hold-mass scale painted green / amber / red — at this mass a competent chute deploy lands soft, hard, or destructive — and GDD 2.6 makes cargo value fight the pilot on the ride down.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Touchdown speed is 3.12 empty, 3.81 half hold, 4.40 full hold, against a 5.0 m/s soft line — and it is identical at all three sample altitudes, because the canopy makes touchdown a pure function of mass. No legal 6-slot hold lands anything but soft: the amber and red zones of the scale have nothing to paint except the module (18.58 m/s at the starter canopy). soft_landing is true in every non-module cell at every band. The square-root mass law is confirmed exactly, and is worth recording as a claim that holds: 3.12 x sqrt(209.1/105) = 4.403 against a measured 4.40, and the module's 3.12 x sqrt(3705/105) = 18.53 against a measured 18.58. The grid satisfies greed_costs_something in only 22.8% of configurations, so this is a narrow target — but the coupling is real and merely under-scaled here.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"climb_heat_lesson_never_fires\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"claim\": \"GDD 2.2 makes the ascent the player's first, safe lesson in reading the heat gauge: 583 m/s crossed, 41.8 seconds above threshold, peaking at 47.5 bar units. balance_notes already flag the lesson going quiet at ~23 against a 222 capacity (10% of bar, where the GDD wanted 23%).\",\n      \"measured\": \"climb_peak_heat is 0.0 at all three samples, and climb_survivable=true is therefore a vacuous pass — the ship never reaches 583 m/s because it is drag-limited near 60-70 m/s and crashes at ~5 km. Any reachability or launch-survivability check reading these fields will report success on a config whose first flight never leaves the lower atmosphere. Note this is exactly GDD 4.4's third risk inverted: the starter shield's capacity is unchosen, and here the ascent gives no evidence to choose it from.\",\n      \"severity\": \"significant\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"claims_that_hold\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3\",\n      \"claim\": \"Two params claims are independently checkable against flight: landing.descent_speed_full_hold_ms 4.4 with a stated canopy of 72 m2 at Cd 1.8, and ablation.heat_index 100 / 124 / 158.\",\n      \"measured\": \"Both hold. The parachute is flown from the stated area rather than solved backwards — independent=true — and measured_full_hold_ms is 4.40 against a claimed 4.40, delta 0, spread across bands 0. The heat index reproduces under the committed-entry family: full-hold plunge peaks of 137.3 / 173.4 / 223.6 normalise to 100 / 126 / 163 against the claimed 100 / 124 / 158, within 3%. Also confirmed as a structural claim: the balance notes' assertion that the module's capacity window is only ~6% wide is correct even though its absolute numbers are not — the measured window between the module's best skim (567.0) and its coolest plunge (603.4) is 6.4% wide.\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"kind\": \"tuning\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"proposed_changes\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.dry_mass_kg\",\n      \"current\": 105,\n      \"proposed\": 600,\n      \"reason\": \"Raises the staged ballistic coefficient from 21.4 to 122 kg/m2 — above the sweep's own 50 kg/m2 floor below which aerobraking cannot exist, and matching the Researcher's assumed staged dry mass — which is the single quantity behind the crashed ascent, the module's 3.4x heat overshoot, and the 16x parachute ladder; it costs the audited full-hold-doubling rule, which cannot be satisfied against a 17.36 kg/slot catalog without either ~100 kg pieces or a lighter module (a GDD/catalog decision, not a value).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.thrust_n\",\n      \"current\": 5200,\n      \"proposed\": 26000,\n      \"reason\": \"A 600 kg dry ship at 5200 N cannot leave the pad (TWR 0.58); 26,000 N restores liftoff TWR to 2.89, near the 3.3 the current numbers were authored for.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.fuel_capacity_kg\",\n      \"current\": 70,\n      \"proposed\": 400,\n      \"reason\": \"Holds the mass ratio at 1000/600 so the ideal delta-v stays 2,213 m/s — unchanged from today — with the whole gain coming from drag rather than from a bigger budget, which keeps GDD 2.5's tank-upgrade progression beat intact.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"flight.fuel_burn_kgs\",\n      \"current\": 1.2,\n      \"proposed\": 6.0,\n      \"reason\": \"Keeps exhaust velocity at thrust/burn = 4,333 m/s so the delta-v above is real and burn time stays 67 s; changing thrust without this silently re-specifies the engine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"reentry.heat_capacity\",\n      \"current\": 222,\n      \"proposed\": 575,\n      \"reason\": \"The only capacity band that satisfies all four flown constraints at once — above the module's best skim (567.0) so the finale lands, below its coolest plunge anywhere (603.4) so the finale must aerobrake, far above the full hold's worst plunge (223.6, restoring the 61% margin the balance notes intended) and above an unstaged pass (417), which restores the staged/unstaged choice for free; it must be re-derived after the dry-mass change, since every peak here was flown at 105 kg.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"upgrades[6].value\",\n      \"current\": 224,\n      \"proposed\": 585,\n      \"reason\": \"Heat shield tier 1, kept inside the measured (567.0, 603.4) window so no tier ever buys the plunge back.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"upgrades[7].value\",\n      \"current\": 226,\n      \"proposed\": 595,\n      \"reason\": \"Heat shield tier 2, the highest legal value: 595 sits 1.4% under the module's coolest plunge, which is the whole margin the flown physics leaves for a three-tier ladder inside a 6.4%-wide window.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"landing.parachute_area_m2\",\n      \"current\": 72,\n      \"proposed\": 242,\n      \"reason\": \"Preserves GDD 2.3's stated 4.4 m/s full-hold descent speed at the proposed 704 kg loaded mass (sqrt(2*704*9/(1.5*1.8*242)) = 4.40); the two upgrade tiers must be re-derived from the same relation after the mass change.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.optimal_skims.top\",\n      \"current\": 1,\n      \"proposed\": 0,\n      \"reason\": \"The flown argmin at the top of the band is one pass, not two — 29.2% of plate against 55.5% for a full hold — so the params currently assert a cost optimum the simulator contradicts and GDD Revision 3 already withdrew.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ablation.skim_heat_multiplier\",\n      \"current\": [1, 0.71, 0.63, 0.6],\n      \"proposed\": [1, 0.54, 0.54, 0.54],\n      \"reason\": \"Matches the measured saturation after exactly one skim and the band-mean benefit (0.716 / 0.519 / 0.382); the per-band spread is real and large, and properly wants a per-band table the schema does not currently have.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"confidence_notes\": [\n    \"The mass proposal and the capacity proposal interact and must not be flown as one step. Every peak-heat figure I used was flown at 105 kg dry; peak scales roughly as m^0.48 and the module-to-ship mass ratio falls from 17.7x to 7.0x under the proposal, which moves the (567.0, 603.4) window substantially. Fly the flight-block changes first, re-run the verification sweep, then set the capacity ladder from the new committed_descents table. The capacity numbers above are the correct answer for today's mass and the correct method for tomorrow's.\",\n    \"The 70 m/s thrust-drag balance speed and the 59 m/s thrust-minus-weight figure are my arithmetic from the flown params (0.5 x 1.5 x v^2 x 0.4 x 3.5 against 5,200 N), not sweep outputs. The sweep reports only the outcome — apex 5.1 km, crashed on ascent. The mechanism is inference; the failure is measured.\",\n    \"The sweep flies the starter canopy only. Every touchdown figure, including the module's 18.58 m/s, is at 72 m2. The balance notes' claim that the module lands at 4.63 m/s on the tier-2 canopy is unflown, and so is the claim that a pristine delivery is possible only at full parachute upgrade. That is the finale's other half and it has never been measured.\",\n    \"The exploration grid does not contain the crew's own planet — radius 200,000 m, gravity 9, sea-level density 1.5 are none of them grid points (radius runs 800/60,000/160,000/320,000). The satisfaction rates bound what is achievable in the neighbourhood of this design, not on the locked world. Treat my kind:tuning versus kind:design calls as directional rather than proofs; the strong ones are bands_reachable at 93.2% (clearly tuning) and full_hold_lands_soft at 6.5% (a genuinely narrow target the current config happens to hit — do not casually disturb the canopy).\",\n    \"There are two descent families in the sweep and they disagree about whether difficulty rises with altitude. The free-braking family (descents) picks its own braking altitude and shows full-hold peaks falling with altitude — 103 / 98 / 94 — while the committed family with the 8,000 m floor shows them rising — 137.3 / 173.4 / 223.6. I read every heat claim off the committed family because the commit floor is the shipped rule and the free family is a route no player can fly. Anyone reading the other table will reach the opposite conclusion about GDD 2.6's risk curve.\",\n    \"I did not reconcile ablation.cost_curve against flown plate. The params index cost by skims and the sweep reports ablation_pct by pass count, so the two tables are on different conventions; I compared argmin only, which is what optimal_skims asserts. The cost_curve's absolute values are unchecked.\",\n    \"The 12,705,186.8% ablation figure at 25 passes is cycle_toll_growth 1.75 compounding, not a simulator fault — it correctly says feathering is unavailable — but it should be clamped before it reaches a report a human reads.\",\n    \"Unstaged braking was flown at the bottom and middle samples only. The top of the band is untested and will be worse; I did not extrapolate a number for it.\",\n    \"This is a point mass with a perfect pilot. reentry.off_retrograde_penalty is 1 and was never exercised, there is no attitude error, no debris collision, and no EVA — the tether leg of the win condition has still never been flown by anything. GDD 4.5's open question, whether 97 seconds is a workable EVA window, is untouched by this sweep and is worse than untouched here, since the measured window is 0 seconds at every sample. And I cannot tell anyone whether any of this is tense or tedious; that remains the one thing a human has to fly.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":190159,"ttft_stream_ms":2933,"time_to_request_ms":397,"type":"result","duration_ms":279607,"uuid":"b46fccb5-91a5-40c9-8a33-dbe45ade1701"}
